Feature: A commit cannot silently delete a backlog ticket it does not own

  The shared pre-commit hook refuses a commit whose staged changes remove a
  backlog ticket YAML when that ticket survives nowhere else in the staged
  tree and the commit message never names it. Moving a ticket (promote,
  close) and deliberately retiring one both stay allowed; only the silent
  drop is refused.

  Background:
    Given the shared pre-commit hook is installed via core.hooksPath
    And the repository has a tracked backlog ticket YAML for "BL-893"

  # BL-901 ticket-deletion-commit-guard-01
  Scenario Outline: The guard refuses only a deletion that is both unrecoverable and unannounced
    Given a staged change that deletes the backlog ticket YAML for "BL-893"
    And the ticket id "BL-893" <survives> at another staged path
    And the commit message <names> the ticket id "BL-893"
    When the hook runs on that commit
    Then the commit is <outcome>

    Examples:
      | survives     | names     | outcome |
      | survives     | omits     | refused |
      | survives     | names     | allowed |
      | disappears   | names     | allowed |
      | disappears   | omits     | refused |

  # BL-901 ticket-deletion-commit-guard-02
  Scenario: A commit that stages no ticket deletion is untouched by the guard
    Given a staged change that only edits "swarmforge/constitution/articles/engineering.prompt"
    When the hook runs on that commit
    Then the commit is allowed

  # BL-901 ticket-deletion-commit-guard-03
  Scenario: An untracked copy in the working tree does not excuse the deletion
    Given a staged change that deletes the backlog ticket YAML for "BL-893"
    And an untracked copy of that same ticket YAML exists in the working tree
    And the commit message omits the ticket id "BL-893"
    When the hook runs on that commit
    Then the commit is refused

  # BL-901 ticket-deletion-commit-guard-04
  Scenario: The refusal names the ticket and how to proceed
    Given a staged change that deletes the backlog ticket YAML for "BL-893"
    And the commit message omits the ticket id "BL-893"
    When the hook runs on that commit
    Then the refusal message names "BL-893"
    And the refusal message states that naming the ticket in the commit message permits it

  # BL-901 ticket-deletion-commit-guard-05
  Scenario: The pre-existing commit-size guard still runs alongside the new guard
    Given a staged change of 200 files that deletes no backlog ticket YAML
    When the hook runs on that commit
    Then the commit is refused
    And the refusal message comes from the commit-size guard
